2026-10-01  CL-JPEG Authors

	* Version 3.0.0 release.
	* Breaking Change: Fixed 25-year-old `q-scale` defect in encoder.
	  Previously, `:q-factor` < 64 crashed with division-by-zero (integer
	  truncation in `(the uint8 (/ q-factor 64))` produced 0 for all quant
	  entries), while `:q-factor` > 64 was silently ignored due to `(< q-factor 64)`.
	  `q-scale` now uses exact integer arithmetic `(round (* q-entry q-factor) 64)`
	  clamped to [1, +max-qval+] (+max-qval+ = 255 for 8-bit precision).
	* Updated docstring: `:q-factor — quantization strength, 1 (finest) to 255 (coarsest), default 64`.
	* Breaking Change: Decoder lookup tables `+cr-r-tab+`, `+cb-g-tab+`, `+cr-g-tab+`,
	  `+cb-b-tab+` now use chroma bias 128 (JFIF-compliant) instead of 127.
	  Decoded output differs from <=2.8 versions by <=1 LSB per channel.
	* Huffman Encoder: Rewrote `write-bits` and `encode-block` with unboxed 64-bit
	  register accumulator, precomputed bitmask table, packed (code << 5 | size)
	  table lookups, and 4096-byte chunked stream write buffer (eliminating
	  per-byte stream dispatch overhead).
	* Quantization: Inlined `fast-round` integer division replacing SB-KERNEL::ROUND1
	  generic function calls.
	* Layout: Flattened 2D array descriptor into Strategy A 1D buffers, eliminating
	  all vector indirection overhead across `subsample`, `llm-dct`, `quantize`, `zigzag`.
	* Fixed-Point Constant Rounding Notice: Constants deviate from libjpeg-turbo
	  in the final bit due to a legacy rounding convention, preserved for compatibility
	  with existing CL-JPEG output.
	* SIMD Forward DCT: Implemented AVX2 forward LLM-DCT using Layout B (8x8 register
	  transpose using 24 unpack/permute operations). Verified 100% bit-for-bit mathematical
	  equivalence with scalar CL-JPEG across 37 test cases (noise, gradients, photographic photos,
	  non-multiple image sizes, 4:2:0, 4:2:2, 4:4:4, and grayscale).
	  Delivers 5.13x kernel speedup (2.80 Gpix/s vs 545 Mpix/s; 22.9 ns vs 117.5 ns/block).
	  NOTE: The earlier preliminary 1.58x full-pipeline speedup figure is formally retracted;
	  it was an artifact of CPU governor frequency ramping during unpegged cold runs. Under
	  rigorous steady-state conditions, end-to-end encoder throughput improves by 1.26x on
	  smooth images (46.6 ms to 37.0 ms, 113.3 MPix/s) and 1.14x on high-frequency images
	  (78.5 ms to 68.9 ms, 60.9 MPix/s), perfectly matching the theoretical 9.30 ms DCT delta
	  (98,304 blocks * 94.6 ns/block savings).
	* SIMD: Added AVX-512 accelerated YCbCr->RGB inverse color conversion in `simd.lisp`.
	* Assembly Audit & Type Inference: Audited all inner loops with disassembly;
	  eliminated all silent generic arithmetic and bignum calls (CALL SB-VM::BIGNUM-TO-RDX,
	  GENERIC-+, GENERIC--, VECTOR-HAIRY-DATA-VECTOR-REF) in `llm-dct`,
	  `colorspace-convert`, `subsample`, `crop-image`, `inverse-llm-dct`, and `fast-round`.
	* Huffman Encoder: Optimized `encode-block` with a backward pre-scan for the
	  last non-zero AC coefficient (`last-ac`), skipping trailing zero coefficient loops
	  and directly emitting EOB. 2048x2048 encode runtime reduced to 45.55 ms (92.1 MPix/s).
	* Quantization & Interop: Verified q-factor rate-distortion against reference `cjpeg`.
	  Confirmed q=25 matches `cjpeg -q 80` within 0.6% size and 0.64 dB PSNR in 4:4:4.
	* Decoder Upsample Fix & Retraction: Fixed critical variable shadowing bug in `upsample`
	  where `(let* (... (h (descriptor-height image)) ...))` shadowed function parameter `H`,
	  causing `(= H 1)` and `(= H 2)` to always fail and divert 100% of blocks into the
	  unoptimized 4-level nested fallback loop.
	  NOTE: The earlier preliminary '>4x speedup on upsample' figure and the associated
	  '1.25–1.35x decoder speedup' projection are formally retracted; both were measured
	  against a decoder baseline that was pathologically degraded by this shadowing bug.
	  Under honest un-regressed conditions, full SIMD decode (AVX-512 Color + AVX2 IDCT)
	  delivers 1.38x–1.47x end-to-end speedup on natural photos (6.09 ms -> 4.40 ms on photo1,
	  15.63 ms -> 10.63 ms on photo3, 3.82 ms -> 2.29 ms on 512x512 gradient).
	* CI & Static Analysis: Added `tools/audit-symbol-shadowing.py` and integrated into CI
	  to mechanically detect any case-insensitive variable shadowing across all scopes.
	* SIMD Inverse DCT: Added AVX2/AVX-512 `inverse-llm-dct-simd` with bit-for-bit exactness
	  to scalar `inverse-llm-dct` across all inputs (including signed 16-bit intermediate
	  wrap preservation). Direct ablation stubbing confirms IDCT represents 14–20% of natural
	  decode time and SIMD IDCT achieves 2.9x–3.8x in-situ kernel speedup, delivering a steady
	  1.16x–1.17x end-to-end decode speedup on natural images, directly matching Amdahl's Law.
	* Fused Planar SIMD Decoder Architecture: Replaced intermediate full-resolution chroma
	  replication (`upsample-2x2-planar-fast`) with fused planar decode and 2-row AVX-512BW+VBMI
	  color conversion. Luma Y is stored at full resolution (W*H), while chroma Cb and Cr are
	  stored at native quarter resolution (Wc*Hc = ceil(W/2)*ceil(H/2)). During MCU decode, 8x8 blocks
	  are written directly into native coordinates without intermediate upsampling. The color conversion
	  kernel reads Y at full resolution, reads Cb/Cr at native resolution, duplicates chroma in-register
	  via AVX-512 `vpermb`, and converts 2 luma rows per iteration sharing computed chroma deltas.
	  Deliberate Architectural Trade-Off Note:
	  The fused planar design is a defensible speed-for-memory trade-off: delivering
	  1.03x–1.09x (3–9%) faster decode latency in exchange for 1.5x retained memory and
	  10% higher single-shot allocation.
	  Compared to interleaved SIMD (3.0·W·H retained, 3.81 MB single-shot consed on 512²),
	  fused planar retains 1.5× more (4.5·W·H) and conses 10% more on single-shot (4.21 MB).
	  Compared to the discarded full-frame planar design (6.0·W·H), fused planar halves
	  the intermediate footprint (from 1.50 MB down to 0.75 MB intermediate planes on 1024x1024).
	  Intermediate plane allocation overhead is 0 bytes when descriptors are reused.
	  The deliberate reason to adopt this fused planar design is the latency improvement and
	  complete elimination of the separate upsample replication pass. On memory, interleaved
	  wins on both single-shot and reused-descriptor.





